<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bal Gangadhar Tilak</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Lokmanya Tilak, B. G. Tilak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bal Gangadhar Tilak, called Lokmanya, was an Indian nationalist and an early, forceful advocate of swaraj, self-rule, known for the slogan &quot;Swaraj is my birthright and I shall have it&quot;. He founded the weeklies Kesari and Mahratta and, with Lala Lajpat Rai and Bipin Chandra Pal, led the radical wing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>, urging swadeshi and the boycott of foreign goods after the partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. Imprisoned for sedition at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a> from 1908 to 1914, where he wrote the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Gita</a> Rahasya, he later helped found the All <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> Home Rule League.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak.jpg" alt="Bal Gangadhar Tilak" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Internet Archive Book Images · No restrictions · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Young_India;_an_interpretation_and_a_history_of_the_nationalist_movement_from_within_(1916)_(14781987942).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
